{"step_index":1536,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-11T23:56:15Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-12T01:56:15+02:00\nCompleted At: 2026-09-12T01:56:15+02:00\nFile Path: `file:///mnt/home/japake298/project/GraphConjecturing/pipeline/known_relations.py`\nTotal Lines: 510\nTotal Bytes: 23725\nShowing lines 1 to 100\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: \"\"\"\n2: pipeline/known_relations.py — curated known relations for the novelty filter.\n3: \n4: The base table in :mod:`pipeline.novelty` speaks a small symbolic vocabulary\n5: (``alpha``, ``gamma``, ``omega``, …) and covers the classical chains\n6: (Whitney, König–Egerváry, Gallai, …). This module *extends* it with a large,\n7: domain-curated list of known graph-invariant relations over the **domination**\n8: and **zero-forcing** families (``total_domination_number``,\n9: ``zero_forcing_number``, ``annihilation_number``, ``slater``, …) that the base\n10: table does not name — the relations a graph theorist already considers folklore\n11: or published, so that a CEGIS survivor restating one of them is flagged\n12: *known* rather than *novel*.\n13: \n14: The relations are kept here as **human-readable strings** (exactly the form a\n15: domain expert writes them), and parsed at import time into the\n16: ``(lhs, {rhs: coeff}, offset, class, name)`` tuples that\n17: ``pipeline.novelty.KNOWN_THEOREMS`` consumes. Parsing is deliberately\n18: conservative: anything that does not parse cleanly to a linear relation over the\n19: known vocabulary (nonlinear ``ceil`` forms, multi-class hypotheses, unsupported\n20: \"not K_n\" / degree-bounded hypotheses, malformed fragments) is **skipped** and\n21: left *novel* — the safe direction, since wrongly hiding a real conjecture is the\n22: only error that matters here.\n23: \"\"\"\n24: from __future__ import annotations\n25: \n26: import logging\n27: import re\n28: from collections import defaultdict\n29: from typing import Dict, List, Optional, Tuple\n30: \n31: logger = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n32: \n33: # A KNOWN_THEOREMS entry:  (lhs, {rhs_inv: coeff, ...}, offset, class|None, name)\n34: Tuple5 = Tuple[str, Dict[str, float], float, Optional[str], str]\n35: \n36: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n37: # Invariant vocabulary.  Tokens (graphcalc-style column names, plus a couple of\n38: # common spellings) map to the symbolic names used inside the novelty table.\n39: # The classical invariants reuse the base table's short names so the new\n40: # relations *compose* with the existing ones through the LP; the domination /\n41: # zero-forcing family invariants are new and map to themselves.\n42: # ---------------------------------------------------------------------------\n43: \n44: TOKEN2SYM: Dict[str, str] = {\n45:     \"order\": \"n\", \"size\": \"m\",\n46:     \"max_degree\": \"Delta\", \"maximum_degree\": \"Delta\",\n47:     \"min_degree\": \"delta\", \"minimum_degree\": \"delta\",\n48:     \"average_degree\": \"avg_deg\",\n49:     \"clique_number\": \"omega\", \"independence_number\": \"alpha\",\n50:     \"chromatic_number\": \"chi\",\n51:     \"domination_number\": \"gamma\", \"independent_domination_number\": \"ind_dom\",\n52:     \"vertex_cover_number\": \"vertex_cover\", \"matching_number\": \"nu\",\n53:     \"diameter\": \"diam\", \"radius\": \"rad\",\n54: }\n55: \n56: _NEW_INVARIANTS = [\n57:     # domination family\n58:     \"total_domination_number\", \"connected_domination_number\",\n59:     \"restrained_domination_number\", \"outer_connected_domination_number\",\n60:     \"roman_domination_number\", \"double_roman_domination_number\",\n61:     \"two_rainbow_domination_number\", \"three_rainbow_domination_number\",\n62:     \"power_domination_number\", \"sub_total_domination_number\",\n63:     \"edge_domination_number\",\n64:     # independence / matching / cover extras\n65:     \"annihilation_number\", \"residue\", \"square_residue\",\n66:     \"slater\", \"LG_slater\", \"min_maximal_matching_number\", \"min_edge_cover\",\n67:     # zero-forcing family\n68:     \"zero_forcing_number\", \"total_zero_forcing_number\",\n69:     \"connected_zero_forcing_number\", \"positive_semidefinite_zero_forcing_number\",\n70:     # distance / misc\n71:     \"triameter\",\n72:     \"power_min_degree_residue_sum\", \"power_min_degree_annihilation_sum\",\n73:     \"power_2_annihilation_sum\", \"power_3_annihilation_sum\",\n74: ]\n75: for _t in _NEW_INVARIANTS:\n76:     TOKEN2SYM.setdefault(_t, _t)\n77: \n78: # Tokens, longest first, so e.g. ``total_domination_number`` is preferred over a\n79: # spurious ``domination_number`` substring (underscores already block most of\n80: # these, but length ordering is a cheap belt-and-braces).\n81: INV_TOKENS = sorted(TOKEN2SYM, key=len, reverse=True)\n82: \n83: # graphcalc column -> symbol, for extending pipeline.cegis_novelty._GC2TABLE so\n84: # that *generated* conjectures over these invariants parse into Inequalities.\n85: GC_COLUMNS: Dict[str, str] = {t: TOKEN2SYM[t] for t in _NEW_INVARIANTS}\n86: \n87: # Graph-class words we model; anything else in a hypothesis -> skip the relation.\n88: _CLASS_WORDS = {\n89:     \"well-covered\": \"well_covered\",\n90:     \"bipartite\": \"bipartite\",\n91:     \"claw-free\": \"claw_free\",\n92:     \"tree\": \"tree\",\n93:     \"triangle-free\": \"triangle_free\",\n94:     \"cubic\": \"cubic\",\n95:     # order-threshold predicates (n ≥ 3 / n ≥ 4): rule out the smallest graphs\n96:     # on which many \"total\" bounds fail only at K_2.\n97:     \"order_bigger_than_2\": \"order_bigger_than_2\",\n98:     \"order_bigger_than_3\": \"order_bigger_than_3\",\n99: }\n100: _SKIP = object()  # sentinel: hypothesis present but not modellable -> drop\nThe above content does NOT show the entire file contents. If you need to view any lines of the file which were not shown to complete your task, call this tool again to view those lines.\n"}
